// Title: UEFI Persistence Via Wpbbin - FileCreation
// Sigma ID: e94b9ddc-eec5-4bb8-8a58-b9dc5f4e185f
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2022-07-18 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: persistence, stealth, T1542.001
// Description: Detects creation of a file named "wpbbin" in the "%systemroot%\system32\" directory. Which could
//   be indicative of UEFI based persistence method
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate usage of the file by hardware manufacturer such as lenovo (Thanks @0gtweet for the tip)
// References:
//   - https://grzegorztworek.medium.com/using-uefi-to-inject-executable-files-into-bitlocker-protected-drives-8ff4ca59c94c
//   - https://persistence-info.github.io/Data/wpbbin.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/file/file_event/file_event_win_wpbbin_persistence.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceFileEvents
| where FolderPath =~ "C:\\Windows\\System32\\wpbbin.exe"
